@page "/docs/icons"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>Icons · blux-ui</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Icons</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">Any icon of any Iconify collection works by name, and the icons components draw can be replaced by name. The build bundles the icons your app uses into the app via MSBuild, so nothing is requested from a third party when the page runs.</p>

    <h2>Names</h2>
    <p>Three forms name an icon, and <code>BxIcon</code> accepts all of them wherever a name is expected (<code>Name</code>, <code>Icon</code>, <code>LeadingIcon</code> of items, <code>BluxOptions.Icons</code>):</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>i-{collection}-{name}</code> prefixed shorthand: <code>i-lucide-lightbulb</code>, <code>i-ph-house</code>, <code>i-simple-icons-github</code>.</li>
        <li><code>{collection}:{name}</code> is the form of Iconify itself: <code>lucide:lightbulb</code>, <code>ph:house</code>, <code>simple-icons:github</code>.</li>
        <li>A bare name is a Lucide icon, as it always was: <code>lightbulb</code>, <code>chevron-down</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A collection prefix can contain dashes (<code>simple-icons</code>, <code>material-symbols-light</code>), so in the <code>i-</code> form the prefix is the longest one that is the prefix of a known Iconify collection: <code>i-mdi-light-home</code> is <code>mdi-light:home</code>, not <code>mdi:light-home</code>. The <code>prefix:name</code> form never needs that list, and is the one to use for the prefix of your own collection when it contains a dash. Browse the icons of every collection at <a href="https://icones.js.org">icones.js.org</a>.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Any form" Code="@Names" />

    <h2>The built-in set</h2>
    <p>The Lucide icons that components draw, and the ones earlier versions shipped, are embedded in the library (about 75), generated at build time from <code>@@iconify-json/lucide</code>. They work with no setup and no network. Every other icon, Lucide ones included, is bundled when the app is built.</p>

    <h2>Bundled at build time</h2>
    <p>The <code>BluxResolveIcons</code> task runs when your project is compiled. It looks through the <code>.razor</code>, <code>.cshtml</code> and <code>.cs</code> files of the project for icon names written as literals, downloads the ones it does not have from the Iconify API (<code>https://api.iconify.design</code>), keeps them in a cache and generates a C# file, compiled into your assembly, that registers them with Blux at startup. The app serves the icon data as part of its own code: no request, no client bundle to configure, and it works the same in static rendering, Server, WebAssembly and Auto, trimmed or not.</p>
    <p>What the scan recognizes is deliberately narrow, so a stray string never costs a download:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The value of an attribute or property whose name ends in <code>Icon</code>, when it is a plain string literal: <code>Icon="ph:house"</code>, <code>LeadingIcon="i-mdi-home"</code>, <code>new("Home") &#123; Icon = "lucide:house" &#125;</code>.</li>
        <li>The <code>Name</code> of a <code>&lt;BxIcon&gt;</code> and the argument of <code>BxIcons.Named("...")</code>.</li>
        <li>The assignment of an app icon: <code>o.Icons.Close = "ph:x"</code>.</li>
        <li>Any string that is, on its own, <code>i-prefix-name</code> with the prefix of an Iconify collection (<code>"i-mdi-home"</code>), anywhere in the code. The <code>prefix:name</code> form is read only in the places above, never as a standalone string: many collection prefixes are ordinary words (<code>token</code>, <code>memory</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>geo</code>), so a <code>"memory:cache"</code> in your code would otherwise be sent to the API.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A bare name (<code>"book-open"</code>) counts only in the first three places, as a Lucide icon. Not detected: a name built at runtime (<code>$"i-lucide-&#123;x&#125;"</code>), a bare name behind an expression (<code>Icon="@@(open ? "a" : "b")"</code>) and comments are not interpreted. A literal inside an expression is found only in the <code>i-</code> form (<code>open ? "i-ph-eye" : "i-ph-eye-slash"</code>). For the others, list the names in the project file.</p>
    <p>The scan reads the files below the project folder and the <code>BluxIconScanDirectory</code> folders, except <code>bin</code>, <code>obj</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>wwwroot</code>, every folder whose name starts with a dot (<code>.git</code>, <code>.vs</code>, <code>.claude</code>, <code>.idea</code>), folders and files that are links, and files over 1 MiB. When no source, item, option or icon file changed since the last clean build, the task reads nothing and leaves the generated file as it is.</p>

    <h2>Explicit icons</h2>
    <p><code>BluxIcon</code> items declare names the source scanner cannot statically see, bundling them unconditionally. <code>BluxIconScanDirectory</code> adds a folder outside the project to the scan.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Site.csproj" Code="@Explicit" />
    <p>The <code>Icon</code> example of the components below builds its names at runtime and relies on exactly this.</p>

    <h2>Custom collections</h2>
    <p>A folder of <code>.svg</code> files is a collection of your own, the equivalent of <code>icon.customCollections</code>. The prefix is the one you give; the name is the file name in lowercase, with dashes for spaces and sub folders (<code>brand/logo.svg</code> is <code>custom:brand-logo</code>). Every file of the folder is bundled. Use <code>currentColor</code> for the paint of the shapes so the icon follows the text color; the <code>viewBox</code> of the file is kept. A file with a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element is refused (its rules would apply to the whole page), and what is not a drawing is removed (see "What is trusted" below).</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Site.csproj" Code="@Collection" />
    <p>To add an icon from code, <code>Register</code> takes the markup that goes inside the <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> element (what Iconify calls the body) and the size of its drawing area, and <code>RegisterSvg</code> takes the text of a whole SVG file. <code>RegisterSvg</code> sanitizes the file the way the build does; <code>Register</code> takes the body as it is and renders it as written. Both are meant for markup your app owns: never register content from end users.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Register" />

    <h2>Icons of the components</h2>
    <p>Components draw their icons from <code>BluxOptions.Icons</code>. Each property is an icon name with a Lucide default. Set one in <code>AddBluxUi</code> and every component that uses it follows, in every render mode (register the options from code shared by the server and the client):</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@AppIcons" />
    <p>A name that is not a built-in Lucide icon is bundled because the assignment is a literal. Setting the value from a variable needs a <code>BluxIcon</code> item. The values become read-only once a component has drawn an icon.</p>
    <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-default" data-testid="icon-keys">
        <table class="w-full min-w-xl text-start text-sm">
            <caption class="sr-only">Icons of BluxOptions.Icons</caption>
            <thead class="bg-muted text-highlighted">
                <tr><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Property</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Default</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Used for</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Keys)
                {
                    <tr class="border-t border-default">
                        <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2 text-start font-mono text-xs font-medium text-highlighted">@row.Name</th>
                        <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-muted">@row.Default</td>
                        <td class="px-3 py-2 text-muted">@row.Meaning</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What is trusted</h2>
    <p>The data of an Iconify icon comes from a third party (the API, or a server you host, or a cache several projects share), and the build compiles it into your assembly where it is rendered as markup. So every icon body, whatever its source (API, cache, folder of files, <code>RegisterSvg</code>), is read as XML (no DTD, no external entity) and written again from an allowlist before it is generated: the drawing and structure elements of SVG (<code>g path circle ellipse line polyline polygon rect text tspan defs symbol use linearGradient radialGradient stop pattern clipPath mask marker filter</code> and its <code>fe*</code> primitives except <code>feImage</code>) and the animation elements (<code>animate animateTransform animateMotion set</code>), with their geometry and presentation attributes. Everything else is dropped with its content: <code>script</code>, <code>foreignObject</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>image</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>desc</code>, other namespaces; links (<code>a</code>) keep what they wrap. Every <code>on*</code> attribute is dropped, <code>href</code> only stays when it points to an id of the same icon, a <code>url()</code> only when it does too, a value with <code>javascript:</code> or <code>data:</code> (after entities are decoded) is dropped, and an animation that could change a link or a handler is dropped. Markup that cannot be read as XML is not guessed at: the icon is left out and reported.</p>
    <p>The ids inside an icon (gradients, clip paths, the animations that start after another) are global to the page, so every drawing of an icon gets its own: <code>id="a"</code> becomes <code>id="a-bx1"</code>, with each reference rewritten. An icon with no id is rendered exactly as it is.</p>

    <h2>Icons that are not found</h2>
    <p>An icon that cannot be resolved at run time renders an empty icon carrying <code>data-bx-missing-icon="name"</code>, so it is easy to find in the page (<code>[data-bx-missing-icon]</code> in the browser's search). At build time, a name in one of the first three places above that does not exist in a known collection, and any <code>BluxIcon</code> item that does not exist, is a warning in a Debug build and an error in a Release build (<code>BluxIconsFailOnError</code> overrides either), with the file and line. A bare name or a standalone string that is not found is only mentioned in the detailed build log, because it may be an icon you register yourself.</p>

    <h2>Cache, CI and offline builds</h2>
    <p>Every icon is cached on its own in <code>obj/blux/icons-cache</code>, so a later build asks the API only for the icons it lacks, and an icon the API reports as not found is remembered too, as is a collection it does not have (a misspelled prefix is answered once per cache window, not at every build, and is reported as an unknown collection rather than as a network failure). An answer that neither has the icon nor lists it as not found is a failed download and is never remembered. A cached icon is trusted for 30 days (<code>BluxIconsCacheDays</code>); when the network fails the cached one is used whatever its age. The cache keeps what the API answered, with a format version: sanitizing happens when the file is generated, so an update of Blux.UI that tightens it also applies to icons already cached, and entries of an older format are asked again when the network is there. Several projects (and target frameworks) can share one cache folder, files are replaced atomically; a folder that cannot be written is a warning (<code>BLUXICON004</code>), never a lost icon. With <code>BluxOffline=true</code> the network is never touched and every icon must be in the cache, so keep <code>BluxCacheDir</code> between CI runs. The first build of a project needs network access to <code>api.iconify.design</code>, or a self-hosted API set with <code>BluxIconsApi</code> (an https address).</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Site.csproj" Code="@Properties" />

    <h2>Auto render mode</h2>
    <p>A Blazor Web App renders a component of its client (WebAssembly) project on the server before any client code ran. The generated file of each project declares its registration with an assembly attribute, and Blux registers every bundle of the app, those of the entry assembly's references included, before the first icon is looked up, so an icon found in the client project is there when the server renders it, and <code>i-custom-logo</code> reads the same whichever assembly renders first. Declare <code>BluxIconCollection</code> and <code>BluxIcon</code> items in the project that holds the components that use them.</p>

    <h2>Turn it off</h2>
    <p><code>BluxIcons=false</code> disables the feature: nothing is scanned, downloaded or generated (a file left by an earlier build is removed), and only the built-in Lucide icons and the icons registered in code are available. <code>BluxIconsScan=false</code> keeps the <code>BluxIcon</code> items and the custom collections but does not read your sources.</p>

    <h2>Migrating from the dictionary</h2>
    <p><code>BluxOptions.Icons</code> was a dictionary from a name to the <code>d</code> of a single path, drawn as a round 2px stroke. It is now the object above. <code>BxIcons.Close()</code>, <code>Check()</code>, <code>ChevronDown()</code> and the other helpers are gone: use <code>BxIcons.Named("x")</code> or an <code>&lt;BxIcon&gt;</code>.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Before and after" Code="@Migration" />
    <p>The markup of an icon is now stored whole, so the stroke is part of the icon instead of part of the component: give a registered path its own <code>fill</code> or <code>stroke</code>.</p>

    <h2>Modes</h2>
    <p>Icons are drawn as inline <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> elements. CSS mask mode is intentionally omitted: inline SVG gives full Blazor control, crisp scaling, and native multicolor icon support with zero extra stylesheet rules.</p>
</div>

